speechtext:
That prophecy has been fulfilled a good many times since Jefferson's day. And still there are many people who ought to be doing something about the situation, but who spend their time ridiculing those of us who are trying to think of the people and their welfare instead of power and position.
Right now this country is facing a very serious threat. There are $7 billion of purchasing power in the form of bank deposits, all of which represent loans to borrowers from the banks. Unsaleable surpluses of our production are beginning to appear. Some of our foreign markets are showing a disposition to slacken off. One of these days the banks will begin to call in their loans, and by their actions will destroy many hundreds of millions ' of dollars of our purchasing power just as they did in the years 1929 to 1933. They could bring on a depression that would make the hungry thirties look like a very pale and washed-out copy. That power is still in their hands. What is the government doing about it? What measures is it bringing forward as a cushion against depression? It is only government that can provide the protection the people need against another bust. It is because the Liberal government has done nothing but ridicule those of us who see the danger and advocate specific measures to meet the situation that I wish to move an amendment to the amendment. I move, seconded by the hon. member for Lethbridge (Mr. Blackmore), that the amendment be amended by inserting between the word "address" and the word "we" the following:
We regret that Your Excellency's advisers have shown such studied indifference to the Social Credit financial proposals put before this house from time to time for bringing social security and freedom to every Canadian, and economic stability and prosperity to the country as a whole;
We also regret that Your Excellency's advisers have demonstrated such timorous diffidence in their failure to take up Social Crediters' oft-repeated challenge to debate in this house and show honest cause why the social credit proposals would not bring widespread security, happiness and contentment to the Canadian people;
We regret, further, that Your Excellency's advisers have failed to bring forward legislative and administrative provision for maintaining purchasing power at a level which would make possible the equitable distribution of our total production and thus cushion the Canadian economy against the onset of depression, therefore . . .
